From uf14d4c9d01f43388 at groups.google.com  Thu Jun 13 22:46:13 2019
From: uf14d4c9d01f43388 at groups.google.com (Matthieu Stigler)
Date: Thu, 13 Jun 2019 22:46:13 +0000
Subject: Re: [D-RUG]: Binned data, finding 25% of total and finding in which bin is this 25% total (new to R)
Message-ID: <GeYbT2qUqGk.ldbJ-msrBAAJ@davis-rug.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <GeYbT2qUqGk.gK8t8ggiBAAJ@davis-rug.groups.google.com>
References: <GeYbT2qUqGk.gK8t8ggiBAAJ@davis-rug.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/davis-rug/c/GeYbT2qUqGk/m/ldbJ-msrBAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

An approach could be to compute percentage then use cumulative sum (cumsum), then subset for first row with cumulated percentage >=25%. You might want to use arrange() for pre-sorting, and group_by if you have multiple groups

library(tidyverse)
df<- data.frame(bin = seq(1, 20, by=4), count = c(7,5,2,6,9)) %>%
  mutate(perc = 100* count/sum(count),
         perc_cumul = cumsum(perc))

df
#>   bin count      perc perc_cumul
#> 1   1     7 24.137931   24.13793
#> 2   5     5 17.241379   41.37931
#> 3   9     2  6.896552   48.27586
#> 4  13     6 20.689655   68.96552
#> 5  17     9 31.034483  100.00000

df %>%
  filter(perc_cumul>=25) %>%
  head(1)
#>   bin count     perc perc_cumul
#> 1   5     5 17.24138   41.37931
<sup>Created on 2019-06-13 by the [reprex package](https://reprex.tidyverse.org) (v0.3.0)</sup>

